1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,682
Hi, this is Krishna Chaitanya.

2
00:00:03,161 --> 00:00:07,582
Today, I want to talk about a problem
I keep running into with AI agents.

3
00:00:08,331 --> 00:00:12,072
The final answer looks right, but
the agent got there the wrong way.

4
00:00:12,922 --> 00:00:15,629
I'll show you how to see it
and how to fix the right thing.

5
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:20,163
And this talk is about you
cannot fix a prompt you cannot

6
00:00:20,165 --> 00:00:22,734
see fail A little about me.

7
00:00:23,174 --> 00:00:27,822
I'm a software engineer, and I currently,
build AI agents for incident response.

8
00:00:28,412 --> 00:00:30,367
I work on how we observe agents.

9
00:00:30,992 --> 00:00:34,024
Earlier this year, I contributed
the plan operation to the

10
00:00:34,058 --> 00:00:36,578
OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions.

11
00:00:37,215 --> 00:00:39,174
This talk is built on that piece.

12
00:00:39,492 --> 00:00:44,689
I also built Agent Telemetry, a benchmark
for finding faults in agent traces.

13
00:00:45,131 --> 00:00:50,473
It was published at ACM AIware
this year And, here's what we'll

14
00:00:50,515 --> 00:00:52,677
cover in the next 20 minutes or so.

15
00:00:53,268 --> 00:00:58,614
First, why agent prompts are hard to
debug, then how to make the agent's

16
00:00:58,628 --> 00:01:04,662
plan visible, followed by a demo with
screenshots from a real run, and lastly,

17
00:01:04,793 --> 00:01:09,740
how to turn what you see into a better
prompt So let's start with the problem.

18
00:01:10,557 --> 00:01:12,140
it starts with a single prompt.

19
00:01:12,358 --> 00:01:13,323
The loop is simple.

20
00:01:13,631 --> 00:01:17,475
You change the prompt, you read
the output, you decide if it got

21
00:01:17,491 --> 00:01:19,117
better, then you do it again.

22
00:01:19,639 --> 00:01:21,977
You can do 10 rounds of
this in a few minutes

23
00:01:22,977 --> 00:01:24,823
Agents break that loop.

24
00:01:25,222 --> 00:01:31,199
An agent doesn't just answer, it makes a
plan, it calls tools, and then it answers.

25
00:01:31,751 --> 00:01:35,331
And when something goes wrong, you
still only see the final answer.

26
00:01:35,955 --> 00:01:37,656
You don't see which tools it picked.

27
00:01:37,737 --> 00:01:40,882
You don't see the step where
it took a shortcut or skipped

28
00:01:40,902 --> 00:01:42,054
something you asked for.

29
00:01:42,784 --> 00:01:47,486
So you change the prompt, run it again,
and hope maybe the answer gets better.

30
00:01:48,410 --> 00:01:50,031
Maybe it gets better for the wrong reason.

31
00:01:50,472 --> 00:01:55,909
You can't tell, and you're tu- tuning
a plan you cannot really see And here's

32
00:01:55,910 --> 00:01:57,471
the case that worries me the most.

33
00:01:57,727 --> 00:02:01,024
Sometimes the answer is
right, but the plan was wrong.

34
00:02:02,304 --> 00:02:03,322
The agent got lucky.

35
00:02:03,653 --> 00:02:06,081
Your answer check passes, so you trust it.

36
00:02:06,662 --> 00:02:10,598
Next time, the same plan gives you a
wrong answer and nothing warned you

37
00:02:11,598 --> 00:02:14,311
So let me make this concrete
with a small example.

38
00:02:14,877 --> 00:02:16,663
I'll use it for the rest of the talk.

39
00:02:17,126 --> 00:02:18,094
It's a release helper.

40
00:02:18,562 --> 00:02:21,903
You ask it one question: Is
this release ready to ship?

41
00:02:22,299 --> 00:02:23,382
It has three tools.

42
00:02:23,864 --> 00:02:26,428
Read summary, which returns
yesterday's summary.

43
00:02:26,845 --> 00:02:28,550
It's fast, but it's old.

44
00:02:29,454 --> 00:02:34,851
Read manifest returns today's list
of required checks, and read checks

45
00:02:35,055 --> 00:02:37,346
returns today's result for those checks.

46
00:02:38,039 --> 00:02:38,868
The rules are simple.

47
00:02:39,346 --> 00:02:41,782
Say ready if every required check passes.

48
00:02:42,291 --> 00:02:44,115
Say blocked if one fails.

49
00:02:45,023 --> 00:02:47,792
Say unknown if you can't get the evidence.

50
00:02:48,418 --> 00:02:52,596
So the right way to answer is
to read the manifest first and

51
00:02:52,596 --> 00:02:54,833
then read the checks it lists

52
00:02:55,833 --> 00:02:58,062
So here are two plans
the agent could make.

53
00:02:58,375 --> 00:03:01,201
Plan A, reads the summary and says ready.

54
00:03:01,699 --> 00:03:05,780
Plan B, reads the manifest, then
reads the checks and says ready.

55
00:03:06,483 --> 00:03:10,177
Today, both answers are right, so
the answer check passes for both.

56
00:03:10,871 --> 00:03:12,804
But only plan B did the work.

57
00:03:13,308 --> 00:03:15,204
Plan A trusted yesterday's summary.

58
00:03:15,670 --> 00:03:18,345
Now imagine tomorrow a
security check fails.

59
00:03:19,049 --> 00:03:21,443
Plan B reads it and says blocked.

60
00:03:21,725 --> 00:03:27,237
Plan A still says ready because it
never looked So how do we see the plan?

61
00:03:27,685 --> 00:03:28,593
We use tracing.

62
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:30,393
There are two words to know.

63
00:03:30,876 --> 00:03:34,224
A trace is one run of your
agent from question to answer.

64
00:03:34,838 --> 00:03:37,393
A span is one step inside that run.

65
00:03:37,788 --> 00:03:41,743
It has a name, a start time, an
end time, and some attributes.

66
00:03:42,339 --> 00:03:46,412
Attributes are just key value facts about
the step, like which tool was called.

67
00:03:47,113 --> 00:03:50,688
This is a real trace from the
demo shown in Jaeger, which is

68
00:03:50,689 --> 00:03:52,296
a free open source trace viewer.

69
00:03:52,936 --> 00:03:54,945
The top row is the whole agent run.

70
00:03:55,602 --> 00:03:59,705
Under it, there's a plan span and
then one span for each tool call

71
00:04:00,705 --> 00:04:04,001
OpenTelemetry has a set of
shared names for AI systems.

72
00:04:04,468 --> 00:04:07,309
They're called the GenAI
semantic conventions.

73
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:10,275
They now include an operation called Plan.

74
00:04:10,984 --> 00:04:12,436
That's the part I contributed.

75
00:04:12,828 --> 00:04:19,052
A plan span records a few things: the
operation name, which is plan, the

76
00:04:19,054 --> 00:04:21,392
agent's name, and the plan itself.

77
00:04:21,921 --> 00:04:24,667
For the plan, I used my own attribute.

78
00:04:25,354 --> 00:04:29,010
In the demo, it's a list of
tools the agent decided to call.

79
00:04:30,137 --> 00:04:34,257
Two notes, these conventions are still
marked development, so pin the version

80
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:39,611
you use, and a plan span records the
plan your app got back from the model.

81
00:04:40,154 --> 00:04:44,536
It doesn't read the model's mind, and
that's fine because the plan is a thing

82
00:04:44,537 --> 00:04:48,393
we want to check So what counts as a plan?

83
00:04:48,969 --> 00:04:50,009
It depends on your app.

84
00:04:50,356 --> 00:04:53,369
It might be a planner call where
you ask the model for a list

85
00:04:53,369 --> 00:04:54,842
of steps and then run them.

86
00:04:55,414 --> 00:05:01,056
It might be a step where the model picks
its tools before calling any, or it

87
00:05:01,056 --> 00:05:05,842
might be a routing step where the agent
picks which sub-agent handles the task.

88
00:05:06,795 --> 00:05:10,888
Add a plan span where your app has
a clear planning step like these.

89
00:05:11,537 --> 00:05:15,722
If planning and acting happen in
the same model call, start with

90
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:17,946
tool spans and add the span later

91
00:05:18,946 --> 00:05:21,135
Recording the plan takes a few lines.

92
00:05:21,681 --> 00:05:25,443
Wrapping your planning step in
a span, set the operation name

93
00:05:25,753 --> 00:05:28,020
to plan, and set the agent name.

94
00:05:28,657 --> 00:05:32,403
Call your model to make the plan,
then save the plan on the span.

95
00:05:33,368 --> 00:05:38,323
After that, each tool call gets its own
span with the list, with the operation

96
00:05:38,325 --> 00:05:40,575
name, execute tool, and the tool's name.

97
00:05:41,176 --> 00:05:43,292
And keep what you save very small.

98
00:05:43,578 --> 00:05:46,277
A list of steps or tool names is enough.

99
00:05:46,323 --> 00:05:49,751
You don't need to store the whole
prompt or, a long explanation.

100
00:05:50,751 --> 00:05:54,338
And, once the plan is recorded,
you can check it like any test.

101
00:05:54,527 --> 00:05:56,929
For the release helper,
the check is short.

102
00:05:57,561 --> 00:05:59,387
Read the tools from the plan span.

103
00:05:59,773 --> 00:06:01,936
Do they include the
manifest and the checks?

104
00:06:02,365 --> 00:06:03,633
Is the manifest right?

105
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:05,700
If yes, then it's a pass.

106
00:06:05,794 --> 00:06:06,967
If not, it's a fail.

107
00:06:07,577 --> 00:06:11,239
And if there's no plan span at
all, then the result is unknown.

108
00:06:11,658 --> 00:06:15,445
I never count a missing span, as
a, sorry, a missing plan as a pass.

109
00:06:15,934 --> 00:06:20,689
This runs next to the answer check,
and, it doesn't really replace it.

110
00:06:22,289 --> 00:06:24,253
So, why does a shared name matter?

111
00:06:25,085 --> 00:06:29,747
Every agent framework logs planning
its own way, if it logs it at all.

112
00:06:30,137 --> 00:06:34,890
And, with one shared name, one query
finds every plan across frameworks,

113
00:06:34,925 --> 00:06:36,442
prompt versions, and models.

114
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:39,674
I measured this gap in my
agent telemetry benchmark.

115
00:06:40,013 --> 00:06:44,575
When I ran it, standard OpenTelemetry
and the GenAI conventions caught, six of

116
00:06:44,576 --> 00:06:49,093
the 14 fault types, and, planning failure
was one of the eight they missed because

117
00:06:49,097 --> 00:06:51,098
there was no planning span to look at.

118
00:06:51,739 --> 00:06:55,845
With agent specific spans, including
a lesson, including a planning span,

119
00:06:56,271 --> 00:06:58,204
the reference setup caught all 14.

120
00:06:59,180 --> 00:07:02,874
And, that's a controlled benchmark,
but the lesson is simple: If the

121
00:07:02,875 --> 00:07:07,335
plan isn't recorded, you can't
really check it And, now the demo.

122
00:07:07,852 --> 00:07:10,432
I recorded it ahead of time,
so everything you'll see is

123
00:07:10,435 --> 00:07:11,828
a screenshot from a real run.

124
00:07:12,285 --> 00:07:14,607
The demo runs the release
helper five times.

125
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:17,449
I scripted the plan so
every run is repeatable.

126
00:07:17,964 --> 00:07:21,461
And, the pla-- the spans are
real OpenTelemetry spans,

127
00:07:21,488 --> 00:07:22,670
and I send them to Jaeger.

128
00:07:23,214 --> 00:07:26,312
There are no model calls, so it
runs on a laptop with no API key.

129
00:07:26,983 --> 00:07:29,075
For each run, I check four things.

130
00:07:29,511 --> 00:07:31,299
Did the answer match what we expected?

131
00:07:31,730 --> 00:07:35,935
Did the plan include the manifest
and, the checks in that order?

132
00:07:36,770 --> 00:07:38,245
Did every tool call succeed?

133
00:07:38,854 --> 00:07:41,646
And did the agent actually
read today's evidence?

134
00:07:42,646 --> 00:07:43,691
And, here's the run.

135
00:07:44,156 --> 00:07:47,907
One command and five cases,
and one row for each case.

136
00:07:48,207 --> 00:07:53,963
And if you look at the first row, it has,
the cases answer passes, and, the answer

137
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:56,490
is ready, and the answer check passes.

138
00:07:57,104 --> 00:07:58,457
You can see that in the second column.

139
00:07:59,058 --> 00:08:00,404
but the plan check fails.

140
00:08:00,838 --> 00:08:01,719
That is the third column.

141
00:08:03,036 --> 00:08:06,101
And the evidence check also
fails, which is the fifth column.

142
00:08:06,507 --> 00:08:10,806
And the tools worked fine in the first
row, and which is the fourth column.

143
00:08:11,815 --> 00:08:13,647
That's plan A from earlier.

144
00:08:14,659 --> 00:08:18,603
If I only had the answer check,
this run would look perfect.

145
00:08:19,296 --> 00:08:21,232
The other rows are
different kinds of trouble.

146
00:08:22,285 --> 00:08:24,439
revised plan passes everything.

147
00:08:25,081 --> 00:08:28,897
Tool timeout has a good
plan, but a tool error.

148
00:08:30,090 --> 00:08:34,580
Missing plan has no plan span,
and stale summary is what happens

149
00:08:34,590 --> 00:08:36,100
when the shortcut finally bites.

150
00:08:36,454 --> 00:08:41,966
let's open a few of these in, Jaeger
This is the answer passes run in Jaeger.

151
00:08:42,978 --> 00:08:46,226
I click the plans span
to open its attributes.

152
00:08:46,632 --> 00:08:50,410
The operation name is
plan and demo.plan.tools

153
00:08:50,412 --> 00:08:52,502
has one entry, read summary.

154
00:08:53,053 --> 00:08:54,330
That's the bug in one line.

155
00:08:54,746 --> 00:08:57,720
The plan never mentions
the manifest or the checks.

156
00:08:58,045 --> 00:09:01,481
No answer check should-- could show
you this because the answer was right.

157
00:09:01,990 --> 00:09:03,324
Now I know where to look.

158
00:09:03,434 --> 00:09:08,275
The problem is in planning, so the fix is
probably in the prompt or in the context

159
00:09:08,276 --> 00:09:11,495
the agent gets Here's the revised plan.

160
00:09:12,333 --> 00:09:15,207
Same agent, prompt version
is, the second one.

161
00:09:16,217 --> 00:09:18,138
Now demo.plan.tools

162
00:09:18,164 --> 00:09:25,316
has read manifest, then read checks, and
at the bottom you can see two tool spans

163
00:09:25,383 --> 00:09:27,896
that match the plan in the same order.

164
00:09:29,669 --> 00:09:34,208
The plan check passes, the evidence
check passes, the answer is still

165
00:09:34,209 --> 00:09:39,406
ready, but now it's ready for the
right reason Now, a different failure.

166
00:09:39,981 --> 00:09:43,232
Same good plan, but the
manifest tool times out.

167
00:09:43,684 --> 00:09:46,225
You can see the red error
icon on the tool span.

168
00:09:46,943 --> 00:09:50,562
In its attributes, error
is true, and error.type

169
00:09:50,597 --> 00:09:51,441
is TimeoutError.

170
00:09:52,147 --> 00:09:53,600
So the plan check passes.

171
00:09:53,824 --> 00:09:58,128
The agent says unknown, which is the right
thing to say when the evidence is missing.

172
00:09:58,777 --> 00:10:00,577
This is why the plan span matters.

173
00:10:01,051 --> 00:10:05,668
From the outside, a timeout and
a bad plan can look the same.

174
00:10:06,297 --> 00:10:07,393
You don't get a good answer.

175
00:10:08,079 --> 00:10:09,044
Here, it's clear.

176
00:10:09,586 --> 00:10:10,700
don't touch the prompt.

177
00:10:10,891 --> 00:10:15,212
Fix the tool first, whether it's a
timeout or by retrying, whatever it is

178
00:10:16,212 --> 00:10:19,008
And, the next case is a missing plan.

179
00:10:20,514 --> 00:10:22,999
On top is a normal run with a plan span.

180
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:25,776
On bottom, the plan span
is actually missing.

181
00:10:26,615 --> 00:10:28,344
Only the tool spans are there.

182
00:10:29,269 --> 00:10:32,494
The answer check passes, but
the plan check says unknown.

183
00:10:33,264 --> 00:10:35,260
It can't judge a plan it can't see.

184
00:10:35,894 --> 00:10:40,778
I like that it says unknown and not
pass because a missing span means

185
00:10:40,827 --> 00:10:44,483
you need to fix your instrumentation
first before you draw any conclusion

186
00:10:44,512 --> 00:10:49,127
about the prompt And here's the
day the shortcut actually bites.

187
00:10:49,584 --> 00:10:53,817
the old summary still says
ready, but today the checks fail.

188
00:10:54,382 --> 00:10:56,076
The right answer is blocked.

189
00:10:56,682 --> 00:10:58,745
Now, now the answer check fails too.

190
00:11:00,061 --> 00:11:01,274
But look at the plan check.

191
00:11:01,696 --> 00:11:03,044
It failed on both days.

192
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:07,272
It flagged this workflow on the good
day before it ever gave a wrong answer,

193
00:11:07,723 --> 00:11:09,777
and that's the main point of this demo.

194
00:11:10,259 --> 00:11:14,241
The plan check catches the risk early,
and the answer check catches it late.

195
00:11:15,241 --> 00:11:18,728
So, when a run fails, here's
how I decide what to fix.

196
00:11:19,325 --> 00:11:22,709
If the plan is wrong, look at
the prompt and the con-context

197
00:11:22,715 --> 00:11:24,210
the agent had when it planned.

198
00:11:24,730 --> 00:11:28,754
And if the plan is right but
a tool failed, fix the tool,

199
00:11:29,106 --> 00:11:30,831
its timeout, or its retry.

200
00:11:31,589 --> 00:11:35,466
Changing the prompt won't help
because if the plan is right but

201
00:11:35,466 --> 00:11:39,211
the tool did something else, look
at the code that runs the plan.

202
00:11:39,466 --> 00:11:43,653
And, if there is no plan span, you
need to first fix your instrumentation.

203
00:11:44,243 --> 00:11:48,915
And, yeah, yeah, the trace tells you
which of these, states you're in, and

204
00:11:48,915 --> 00:11:50,413
that alone saves a lot of guessing.

205
00:11:52,264 --> 00:11:55,797
And, for our planning bug, the
trace points to a prompt change.

206
00:11:56,244 --> 00:11:58,611
So here are the two prompts
for the-- from the demo.

207
00:11:59,011 --> 00:12:03,412
Version one says, "Check re-release
readiness and return ready or blocked."

208
00:12:03,937 --> 00:12:07,581
It never says where the evidence
should come from, so reading an old

209
00:12:07,582 --> 00:12:09,264
summary is a fair way to follow it.

210
00:12:09,850 --> 00:12:13,462
Version two names the source, "Read
the current release manifest."

211
00:12:13,967 --> 00:12:17,700
It asks for every required check,
and it says what to do when evidence

212
00:12:17,700 --> 00:12:20,071
is missing, "Return unknown."

213
00:12:20,692 --> 00:12:26,380
Each change maps to something we saw
in the trace Then the test, then test

214
00:12:26,384 --> 00:12:28,158
the change itself like an experiment.

215
00:12:28,579 --> 00:12:33,278
Keep everything else the same: same
model, same tools, same test cases.

216
00:12:34,015 --> 00:12:37,230
If you change three things at
once, you won't know what helped.

217
00:12:37,927 --> 00:12:39,731
Run each prompt many times.

218
00:12:40,265 --> 00:12:43,240
Models are random, and one
good run doesn't prove much.

219
00:12:43,935 --> 00:12:46,356
Score the answer and the plan separately.

220
00:12:46,925 --> 00:12:49,899
One combined score hides
why things changed.

221
00:12:51,371 --> 00:12:53,605
And keep every failure as a test case.

222
00:12:55,101 --> 00:12:57,360
Answer passes stays in my suite for good.

223
00:12:57,919 --> 00:12:58,829
One call-out.

224
00:12:59,289 --> 00:13:01,880
In this demo, the version
two plan is scripted.

225
00:13:02,234 --> 00:13:05,796
With the real model, this is where you
measure how often the plan comes out right

226
00:13:06,796 --> 00:13:10,625
And, if you want to try this on your own
agent this week, here's where I'd start.

227
00:13:11,046 --> 00:13:13,320
Pick one agent that has
a clear planning step.

228
00:13:13,878 --> 00:13:15,773
Write down the evidence the task needs.

229
00:13:15,983 --> 00:13:18,156
Wrap the planning step in a plan span.

230
00:13:18,927 --> 00:13:23,560
Add one plan check next to your answer
check, and keep every failure as a test.

231
00:13:24,109 --> 00:13:25,008
And, that's it.

232
00:13:25,314 --> 00:13:29,832
You don't need to trace everything on
day one, and one plan span and one check

233
00:13:29,832 --> 00:13:35,167
already give you a much better loop And,
to sum up, the answer tells you how a

234
00:13:35,167 --> 00:13:39,363
run ended, the plan tells you how it
